      "heading": "The threshold sweep: ROC, AUC and PR curves",
      "narration": "Here is a fact that reorganizes the whole topic. Every precision and recall pair you can quote belongs to one particular threshold. Move the threshold and you get a different pair. So a single precision-recall pair does not describe your model — it describes your model at one setting. What describes the model is the whole curve you trace as you sweep the threshold from one down to zero. The classical version of this is the ROC curve, which plots the true positive rate, which is just recall, against the false positive rate, which is the fraction of negatives you wrongly flagged. At a threshold of one you flag nothing and sit at the origin. At a threshold of zero you flag everything and sit at the top right. In between, the curve bulges toward the top-left corner, and the more it bulges the better your model separates the classes. Summarize it by the area underneath, the AUC, and that number has a genuinely beautiful interpretation: AUC is the probability that a randomly chosen positive example receives a higher score than a randomly chosen negative one. So nought-point-five is a coin flip — your model has no ranking ability at all — and one is a perfect ranking. That interpretation is worth memorizing, because it makes clear what AUC does and does not measure: it measures ranking quality, entirely independent of any threshold you pick and independent of the prevalence of the classes. That independence is exactly why AUC is excellent for comparing two models to each other, and exactly why it can mislead you about whether either model is useful. Consider a rare class: one positive in a thousand. Your false positive rate can be a comfortable-looking one percent, which puts the ROC curve up in a lovely arc — but one percent of a thousand negatives is ten false alarms for every one real case, so your precision is under ten percent and the system is unusable in practice. ROC did not lie; it answered a question about ranking while you needed an answer about usefulness. So the rule for imbalanced problems: use the precision-recall curve instead. It plots precision against recall across the same threshold sweep, and because precision has false positives in its denominator rather than true negatives, it does not get quietly rewarded for the enormous, trivial mass of correct negatives. On rare-class problems the PR curve degrades honestly and the ROC curve flatters. When the classes are balanced, the two tell the same story and you may use either.",
