      "heading": "Measuring honestly: cross-validation, leakage and drift",
      "narration": "Finally, the discipline that makes all of these numbers mean something. A single train-test split gives you one number with no error bar, and if your test set is small, that number moves by several points depending on which rows happened to land in it. K-fold cross-validation gives you k numbers instead. Report their mean, and report their spread, and adopt this rule: never declare one model better than another when the gap between them is smaller than the spread across folds. That rule alone will save you from a great deal of confident nonsense. Two details on how you build folds. Stratify them, so that each fold preserves the overall class balance — with a two percent positive class and ten folds, an unstratified split can easily hand you a fold containing almost no positives, and the metric on that fold is noise. And respect structure: never shuffle time-ordered data, and keep all the rows belonging to one patient, one customer, one ship inside a single fold. Now leakage, which is the silent killer of this entire field. Leakage is when information about the answer reaches your features by a route that will not exist at prediction time. A column computed after the outcome was known. A record ID that happens to be assigned in outcome order. A scaler or a vocabulary fitted on the full dataset before splitting. In every case the model learns something real that it will never have access to in production. The symptom is always the same: a suspiciously excellent score. So build this reflex — when your AUC comes back at nought-point-nine-nine, do not celebrate, investigate. Treat it as a bug report. Ask what your model could possibly be seeing, and check whether each feature would genuinely have been available at the moment of prediction. And last: even a perfectly measured number decays. You trained on last year and you deployed this year, and the world moved underneath the model — customers changed, fraudsters adapted, a supplier changed their format. That is drift, and it is not a failure of your modelling, it is the normal condition of deployed systems. So monitor the live metric, monitor the distribution of the inputs, which usually shifts before the metric visibly does, and plan the retraining schedule before you launch rather than after the complaint arrives. Evaluation is not a milestone you pass on the way to shipping. It is a process that starts at shipping and does not stop.",
